@wtgodbe do you have a suggested fix to address the [`FrameworkReference` issue you mentioned above](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/26788#issuecomment-712505294)❔ I ask in part b/c near-empty `@(FrameworkReference)` items are pretty common and most of the information that eventually ends up in `@(ResolvedTargetingPack)` and `@(ResolvedFrameworkReference)` comes from `@(KnownFrameworkReference)` items. Suspect existing `@(KnownFrameworkReference)` workarounds are either incomplete or need to be applied in a few more places&hellip;

For Helix testing, the general issue is we do _not_ create Directory.Build.props / &hellip;targets files on the Helix agents unless a test project does that. This means tests (the `dotnet-watch` and project template tests in particular) attempt to use whatever targeting pack and shared runtimes are included in the SDK, not what we just built. That's more general than whether or not the SDK recognizes the `net6.0` TFM.

I'm generalizing the infrastructure we use (now) in the on-build-agent template tests to work on the Helix agents. On top of that, we'll need `<TestDependsOnAspNetRuntime>true</TestDependsOnAspNetRuntime>` in `dotnet-watch.Tests.csproj.

@JunTaoLuo and @wtgodbe, please look at the non-Helix test failures.
